WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.473
Desert sand is not one size. It is a mixture of grains, from

2
00:00:03.573 --> 00:00:07.344
fine dust to coarse sand, and no single binder holds all of them.

3
00:00:09.011 --> 00:00:13.835
Calcium-carbonate cementing is strongest on fine-to-medium grains. But the cells cannot

4
00:00:13.935 --> 00:00:17.571
colonise the very finest sand, and the coarsest grains leave pores

5
00:00:17.671 --> 00:00:20.118
too wide to bridge, so it fades at both ends.

6
00:00:20.184 --> 00:00:23.274
This is where the sticky gamma-P-G-A gel earns its place.

7
00:00:23.374 --> 00:00:25.959
It bridges the fine and medium grains, and it is

8
00:00:26.059 --> 00:00:29.541
strongest exactly where cementing cannot reach, at the fine end.

9
00:00:30.107 --> 00:00:33.465
Overlap the two, and fine through medium sand is held by at

10
00:00:33.565 --> 00:00:37.627
least one mechanism. Over the UAE's dune sand, which sits mostly around

11
00:00:37.727 --> 00:00:41.553
two hundred microns, about seven-eighths of the sand mass is bound.

12
00:00:41.653 --> 00:00:44.526
The honest weak point is the coarsest tail, where both

13
00:00:44.626 --> 00:00:47.444
binders fade at once. A two-route crust is a coverage

14
00:00:47.544 --> 00:00:49.756
argument, not a claim to hold every grain.

